.TH ED 1 2026-09-25 "Substrate" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
ed \- line editor (placeholder)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ed
.SH DESCRIPTION
The Substrate
.B ed
is a minimal placeholder, not a working implementation of the
standard line editor.
It takes no options and no file operand, cannot read a file, and
cannot write one.
Any command-line arguments are ignored.
.PP
.B ed
reads commands from standard input, one per line, and looks only at
the first character of each line:
.TP
.B a
Append text.
Subsequent input lines are stored in an in-memory buffer until a line
consisting of a single
.B .
is read.
.TP
.B p
Print every line in the buffer.
.TP
.B w
Print the message
.B "written (mock)"
and do nothing else; the buffer is not saved.
.TP
.B q
Exit.
.PP
Every other command is silently ignored; there is no line addressing,
no error reporting, and no
.B ?
diagnostic.
.B ed
also exits at end of input.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B ed
always exits with status 0.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ex (1),
.BR vi (1)
.SH BUGS
The buffer holds at most 1024 lines; further appended lines are
discarded without warning.
Input lines longer than 1023 bytes are split into several buffer
lines.
Because only the first character of a command is examined, any line
beginning with
.BR q ,
.BR a ,
.BR p ,
or
.B w
is treated as that command.
